[00:19.95] He would see faces in movies, on T.V., in magazines, and in books
[00:27.43] He thought that some of these faces might be right for him
[00:31.81] And that through the years, by keeping an ideal facial structure fixed in his mind
[00:37.76] Or somewhere in the back of his mind
[00:40.41] That he might, by force of will, cause his face to approach those of his ideal
[00:49.67] The change would be very subtle
[00:54.32] It might take ten years or so
[00:59.27] Gradually his face would change its shape
[01:03.38] A more hooked nose
[01:05.10] Wider, thinner lips
[01:09.78] Beady eyes
[01:14.10] A larger forehead
[01:15.94] 
[01:28.53] He imagined that this was an ability he shared with most other people
[01:34.01] They had also molded their faces according to some ideal
[01:38.82] Maybe they imagined their new face
[01:41.48] Would better suit their personality
[01:44.00] Or maybe they imagined that their personality
[01:48.15] Would be forced to change to fit the new appearance
[01:58.46] This is why first impressions are often correct
[02:08.16] Although some people might have made mistakes
[02:13.05] They may have arrived at an appearance
[02:15.99] That bears no relationship to them
[02:19.22] They may have picked an ideal appearance
[02:22.02] Based on some childish whim, or momentary impulse
[02:28.44] Some may have gotten halfway there
[02:33.09] And then changed their minds
[02:34.94] 
[02:47.53] He wonders if he too might have made a similar mistake
[02:51.60] 